# --- Dependencies ---
node_modules/
# GO DEPENDENCY VENDORING, AT MODULE ROOTS ONLY.
#
# This was a bare `vendor/` and it matched any directory of that name at any
# depth — including services/market-data/internal/vendor, which is not vendored
# dependencies at all but this project's own market-data vendor adapter. Its one
# file was therefore excluded from the repository for as long as it existed, a
# fresh clone could not build market-data, and nothing noticed: an untracked file
# cannot fail a comparison against the commit it has no place in.
#
# Anchored and scoped so it still ignores what it was written for.
/vendor/
/packages/*/vendor/
/services/*/vendor/

# --- Build output ---
dist/
build/
*.tsbuildinfo
coverage/

# --- Go ---
bin/
*.exe
*.test
*.out

# --- Environment ---
.env
.env.*
# The TEMPLATES go in; only the filled-in files stay out. Key names are not
# secrets, and a machine nobody can configure is the same class of problem as a
# repository that is not the whole record of what runs. Without the second
# negation, .env.auth.example was silently ignored by the .env.* above — which
# is how three of these came to be missing in the first place.
!.env.example
!.env.*.example

# --- IDE / OS ---
.idea/
.vscode/
*.swp
.DS_Store
Thumbs.db

# --- Logs & temp ---
*.log
tmp/

# --- Local tooling (never pushed to remote / VPS) ---
.commandcode/

# Build artefacts produced on the host by infra/systemd/install.sh.
# Untracked binaries made every deployed-version check report a dirty working
# tree, which would have trained everyone to ignore the one signal that says
# production is running code nobody reviewed.
scheduler-bin/
services/*/server
services/*/backfill
# The lease-race binary guard-verify builds from cmd/leaserace and removes again.
# Listed so that a run which dies before its cleanup cannot make
# repo-complete-verify report an unclassified file.
.guard-verify-race

# go.work.sum WAS IN THE LIST ABOVE, and did not belong there. It is not a build
# artefact: it is the checksum record for the workspace, and go.work itself is
# committed. "The toolchain can regenerate it" is not the same claim as "the
# toolchain will regenerate the same thing" — what it writes depends on which
# module versions it happens to need at the time. If go.work is part of the
# record of what builds here, its sums are part of the same record.

# The build stamp lives inside dist/ and is written by the installer.
# Listed so a future dist/ ignore rule cannot silently take it with it.
!services/*/dist/.build-commit
.dust-verify-build/

# Verbose go test logs written by `make test-go`.
.test-logs/

# Full verifier output kept by `node infra/verify/run-all.mjs`. Kept on disk on
# purpose: the tool it replaced scraped summary lines and threw the output away,
# so a failure that did not repeat could not be looked at afterwards.
.verify-logs/
